- token — the bearer token used for authentication.
- ca.crt — the certificate authority certificate used to verify the API server's TLS certificate.
- namespace — the Pod's namespace.

The token is a credential. Do not print it in logs, commit it to Git, or share it in screenshots.

The API request flow

1. Application in Pod
Reads the mounted token

2. HTTPS request
Sends Authorization: Bearer TOKEN

3. API server authentication
Validates the token, signature, expiry and audience as applicable

4. Authorization
RBAC or another configured authorizer checks the requested action

5. Response
Request is allowed or denied


Authentication and authorization are different:
- Authentication: Who is making the request?
- Authorization: Is that identity allowed to perform the requested action?
A valid ServiceAccount token does not automatically authorize every API request


7. What is RBAC, and how does it work with a ServiceAccount?
RBAC stands for Role-Based Access Control.

A ServiceAccount needs appropriate permissions when an application must access Kubernetes resources. Usually, you configure three things:
1. ServiceAccount: identifies the application.
2. Role: defines allowed actions on resources.
3. RoleBinding: grants that Role to the ServiceAccount.

8. How to create a role :

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader-role
  namespace: dev
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: [""]
    verbs: ["list", "get", "watch"]

apiGroups: [""]	The core API group, which includes Pods
resources: ["pods"]	The resource that can be accessed
verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]	Read individual Pods, list Pods, and watch for changes

root@quixydevops:/home/devopsprod# kubectl get roles -n dev
NAME              CREATED AT
pod-reader-role   2026-10-23T10:36:16Z

9. How to create a rolebinding and bind it to a role (auth we are using SA)

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata: 
  name: pod-reader-rolebinding
  namespace: dev
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: pod-reader
    namespace: dev
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader-role

10. Assign the ServiceAccount to a Pod
Creating an SA and granting permissions are not enough by themselves. The Pod must use that identity.
Example pod.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: pod-reader-test
  namespace: dev
spec:
  serviceAccountName: pod-reader
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:latest

What about Deployments?
Use the same field under the Pod template:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: pod-reader-app
  namespace: dev
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: pod-reader-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: pod-reader-app
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: pod-reader
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx:latest

Both replicas use the same ServiceAccount identity. You can give different workloads different ServiceAccounts, depending on their responsibilities.

If a Pod template changes its ServiceAccount, the Deployment creates replacement Pods through a rollout.

What is automountServiceAccountToken?
By default, Kubernetes generally mounts the assigned ServiceAccount's API credentials into a Pod. If a workload does not need to call the Kubernetes API, you can disable this behavior 

At Pod level:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: frontend
spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:latest


At ServiceAccount level:
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: frontend-sa
  namespace: dev
automountServiceAccountToken: false


The Pod-level setting takes precedence over the ServiceAccount-level setting.

When should you disable it?
- A frontend web application only serves HTTP traffic.
- A static website does not call the Kubernetes API.
- An application uses a separate identity mechanism and does not need a Kubernetes token.
Do not disable the token when the application genuinely relies on the automatically mounted credentials, unless you configure another suitable authentication method.



11. Generate a ServiceAccount token

Pod presents token → API server authenticates it as system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<sa-name> → RBAC decides what it may do.

In modern Kubernetes (v1.24+), creating a ServiceAccount does not create any token. The SA is just an identity object until something asks for a token

When is a token actually generated?

Tokens are created on demand, in three ways:

1. When a Pod uses the SA
Pod created with serviceAccountName: app-sa
        ↓
Pod is scheduled to a node
        ↓
kubelet calls the TokenRequest API on the API server
        ↓
API server signs a JWT (bound to this Pod, with expiry)
        ↓
kubelet writes it into the Pod's projected volume:
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
        ↓
kubelet refreshes it before it expires

The token is never stored in etcd. It is a signed JWT generated at request time, and the API server just verifies the signature later. When the Pod is deleted, the token becomes invalid.

2. Manually, with kubectl create token
kubectl create token app-sa -n default --duration=1h

This calls the same TokenRequest API and prints a short-lived token. Nothing is stored in the cluster either.

3. Manually creating a legacy long-lived Secret token

apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: app-sa-token
  annotations:
    kubernetes.io/service-account.name: app-sa
type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token

Here the token controller sees the Secret and fills in the token field. This one is stored in etcd and never expires, so use it only when an external system can't handle short-lived tokens.

ServiceAccount (SA) is the identity for a Pod, like an employee record. It's stored in etcd.
Token is the credential (proof of identity), like an ID card. It is a JWT signed by the API server.
Role + RoleBinding (RBAC) decide what that identity is allowed to do.
Authentication (who are you) is done by the token. Authorization (what can you do) is done by RBAC.
The API server sees the identity as system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<sa-name>, e.g. system:serviceaccount:sriya:sa.

kubectl get sa shows SECRETS 0, and kubectl get secrets shows nothing.

You create the SA. Only the object is saved in etcd.
You create a Pod with serviceAccountName: sa.
The ServiceAccount admission controller (in the API server) automatically injects a projected volume (kube-api-access-xxxxx) and a mount into the Pod spec.
The Pod is scheduled to a node.
The kubelet on that node calls the TokenRequest API and asks for a token for that SA and Pod, valid about 1 hour.
The API server builds the JWT and signs it with its private key (sa.key on the control plane).
The kubelet writes the token into the Pod's volume on the node.
The volume is mounted in the container at /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/.


It is not in etcd, not in a Secret, and not a Kubernetes object.
It is not stored by the API server. It only verifies the signature using its public key.
It exists only as a file in the Pod's volume on the node, mounted into the container.
Node path: /var/lib/kubelet/pods/<pod-uid>/volumes/kubernetes.io~projected/.
The Pod object stores only the instruction (the volume definition, expirationSeconds), never the token value.


Why a volume is used
A container can only get files from the image, env vars, or volumes.
The token doesn't exist at build time and changes over time, so the image doesn't work.
Env vars are fixed at container start and can't be rotated.
A volume lets the kubelet write the file after the Pod starts and update it later.
The volume is deleted with the Pod, so the token is cleaned up too.

Projected volume vs PV/PVC
Projected volume is a field in spec.volumes of the Pod. It is not a separate object, so kubectl get volumes doesn't exist. It lives and dies with the Pod, and is used to inject the token, CA, and config.
PV/PVC are separate Kubernetes objects (kubectl get pv,pvc). They outlive Pods and are used to store real data.
Other volume types: emptyDir, configMap, secret, projected, persistentVolumeClaim, hostPath.
To see the volume, use kubectl describe pod or kubectl get pod <name> -o yaml

The token is tied to a specific Pod UID.
If the Pod is deleted, the token is rejected immediately, even though exp is a year away.

Container restart (crash, OOM) keeps the same Pod, volume, and token, so nothing changes.
Pod deleted and recreated gives a new Pod UID and a new token, and the old token is dead.
The token path stays the same, so app code doesn't change.
The SA, Role, RoleBinding, and permissions stay the same, because they belong to the SA and not the token.
A bare Pod isn't recreated automatically. A Deployment creates a replacement Pod.


A 401 means authentication failed. A 403 means authenticated but not authorized.


12. Cross-namespace access
A ServiceAccount belongs to one namespace, but RBAC can allow it to access resources in another namespace.

For example:
- ServiceAccount: dev/pod-reader
- Target resources: Pods in prod
- Permission: read Pods in prod
You can create a Role in prod and bind it to the ServiceAccount in dev.

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: prod-pod-reader
  namespace: prod
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: allow-dev-pod-reader
  namespace: prod
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: pod-reader
    namespace: dev
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: Role
  name: prod-pod-reader

The RoleBinding must exist in the namespace containing the resources being accessed. This allows cross-namespace access without giving the ServiceAccount cluster-wide permissions

- ServiceAccounts and Private Image Registries

The problem

When a pod starts, the kubelet on the node must pull the container image. For a public image like nginx, no credentials are needed. For a private registry (ECR, ACR, GCR, Docker Hub private repos, Harbor, etc.), the registry demands authentication, otherwise you get:

ErrImagePull / ImagePullBackOff
Failed to pull image: unauthorized: authentication required

Kubernetes needs a way to hand registry credentials to the kubelet. That’s where imagePullSecrets and ServiceAccounts come in.

The pieces

1. A docker-registry Secret holds the credentials:

bash
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com \
  --docker-username=myuser \
  --docker-password='mypassword' \
  --docker-email=me@example.com \
  -n sriya

This creates a Secret of type kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson.
1. directly on the pod (per workload) under spec field :

imagePullSecrets:
  - name: harbor-docker
You must repeat this in every Deployment, Job, and CronJob.

2. attach it to the ServiceAccount (the clean way)
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: sa
  namespace: sriya
imagePullSecrets:
  - name: regcred

Now every pod that uses this ServiceAccount automatically inherits the imagePullSecrets. The pod spec doesn’t need to mention it at all.

When a pod is created, the ServiceAccount admission controller looks at spec.serviceAccountName (or default if unset). If the pod has no imagePullSecrets of its own, it copies the ones from the ServiceAccount into the pod spec. The kubelet then uses them to authenticate with the registry.

- Same namespace only. The Secret must exist in the same namespace as the ServiceAccount and pod. Secrets don’t cross namespaces, so you need to create regcred in every namespace that needs it.

- Existing pods are not updated. The injection happens at pod creation. After changing the SA, restart the pods (kubectl rollout restart deployment/<name>).

- Pod-level wins. If the pod defines its own imagePullSecrets, the SA’s are not merged in

- Default ServiceAccount trick. Patching the default SA in a namespace makes all pods there (that don’t set a custom SA) able to pull private images

- Don’t confuse this with RBAC. imagePullSecrets is for kubelet to registry authentication. RBAC (Roles/RoleBindings) is for pod to Kubernetes API authorization. They are unrelated even though both hang off the ServiceAccount

- One-line summary
A ServiceAccount can carry imagePullSecrets so that every pod running under it automatically gets the registry credentials needed to pull private images, saving you from repeating the secret in every pod spec.

- TO CHECK --- kubectl auth can-i list pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:my-sa -n dev


*** A Role and its RoleBinding must be in the same namespace. A Role is namespaced and grants permissions only within its own namespace. A RoleBinding can only reference a Role in its own namespace (or a ClusterRole). It cannot reference a Role in a different namespace. ***

*** The ServiceAccount (the subject) can be in any namespace. This is the part that crosses namespaces ***

- Role	          Lives in the namespace where the resources are
- RoleBinding	    Must be in the same namespace as the Role (where access is granted)
- ServiceAccount  Can be in any namespace

roleRef: only kind and name. There is no namespace field here, because it’s implicitly the RoleBinding’s own namespace.
subjects: the SA’s name and namespace. This is where the namespace is mentioned.














